  "query_preview": "..\n\nRest of Today through Friday...\n\nUnsettled pattern over the next 36 hours as a Fall like upper\nsystem approaches from the west and slides just to the north of\nthe area Sunday into Monday. This close approach will keep a\nchance of showers and below normal temperatures over the area\nSunday. But precipitation amounts have been trending lower as the\ntrack of the low to the north puts the forecast area under a\ndownslope flow pattern that will work against significant\nprecipitation potential. \n\nFor tonight areas along the western foothills\n(Gardiner/Livingston/Harlowton) will see the best chances for\nprecipitation, along with areas along the eastern state line. The\nbest chances for a stronger thunderstorm (wind gusts around 50\nmph) will be in the eastern locals through mid-evening. \n\nThere could be a dusting of snow in the Beartooth mountains late \ntonight into early Sunday morning, above 10500 feet, for those \nplanning high elevation recreation activities.\n\nTemperatures Sunday will be 5 to 10 degrees cooler than today with\na good chance for precipitation across the area. The western half\nof the area (Billings and west) will see the best chances for\nprecipitation during the morning, with areas to the east favored\nfor the afternoon. Monsoonal moisture plume will be mainly east of\nthe area Sunday and this combined with the westerly flow aloft\ndownsloping along our mountains continues to trend precipitation\namounts downward. Most areas are looking at a tenth of an inch or\nless Sunday, though a few areas of more organized convection could\nsee up to a quarter of an inch. Winds Sunday afternoon will\nincrease out of the west with gusts in the 30 to 40 mph range\nbecoming common for the afternoon/early evening hours. Given the\nrecent rainfall across the area, do not anticipate fire concerns \nbecoming elevated tomorrow afternoon. \n\nUpper system exits to the east on Monday with temperatures warming\nas westerly winds dry out the atmosphere. Temperatures make it\nback into the low to mid 80s Monday afternoon with plenty of sun\nacross the area. Winds may be a bit breezy to start the afternoon\nover SE Montana, but should diminish by later in the day. \n\nWest to southwest flow will boost temperatures back above normal \nTuesday and Wednesday with highs possibly reaching the lower 90s \nin some locations Wednesday afternoon. There are low precipitation\nchances in the forecast each afternoon and evening mid-week, but\nthis activity looks to produce more wind than rain in general with\nthunderstorm wind gusts becoming an increasing threat especially\nover the western mountains/foothills. \n\nAnother Fall like upper level storm system arrives along the PacNW\ncoast Thursday. This system deepens but doesn't shift eastward\nvery quickly. This will put our area in a persistent southwesterly\nflow with a series of shortwaves moving into the area as low\nlevel winds turn southeasterly advecting in moisture for the area.\nThis is a favorable thunderstorm pattern for our region. How\nactive things will get toward the end of the week will depend on\nhow much moisture gets drawn into the region and how close the\nupper low approaches from the west. Stay tuned to the forecast if\nyou have outdoor plans Thursday into the weekend. Chambers",
